From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] kk: query pools without per-device occlusion and per-process
 timestamp ceilings

Occlusion results lived in at most 16 chunks of 32768 per device (0037):
524288 queries, exactly 128 of vkd3d-proton's occlusion query pools of
4096, so the 129th live one failed to create. The chunk table libkk reads
now has 4096 entries (2^27 queries, 32 GB of results: device memory runs
out first); chunks are allocated separately and the device keeps pointers.
A render pass still writes the chunk of its active query and restarts
with another chunk's visibility buffer when a query of that chunk begins.

Timestamp pools held entries of the process' 32 Metal counter heaps of
4096 (Metal's limits) for their lifetime, so all timestamp pools of every
device in the process (every guest application under virglrenderer)
together held at most 131072 queries: two D3D12 timestamp query heaps of
65536 used them up. Counter heap entries now belong to command buffers:
each takes chunks of 16 entries as it records timestamp writes (samples
are resolved into the pool within the same Metal command buffer) and
returns them when it is reset or freed. Query pools take none, so their
number and size are not limited; only timestamps recorded in command
buffers that are not reset count, and a write finding none left makes its
queries available with 0 (logged once) instead of never.

vkCreateQueryPool failures log their reason in release builds ("MESA:
error: vkCreateQueryPool failed: <reason> (<VkResult>; <type>, <count>
queries, statistics, flags)"): Venus creates query pools asynchronously,
so the guest only saw a CS error at the pool's first use (STEAMAC-2X: Path
of Exile 2, "vkr: failed to look up object ... of type 12", then
"vkCmdResetQueryPool resulted in CS error").

Compute encoder timestamps are written with Metal's precise granularity.
Relaxed granularity (which may sample at encoder boundaries) sampled only
the first 4 timestamps of a compute encoder; the others resolved whatever
their counter heap entry held. With entries per pool that was usually an
earlier sample of the same query, which hid it (vkd3d-proton's
test_query_timestamp_write_after_read passed); with entries reused across
command buffers it is 0 or another query's sample.
